# BigBlueButton API

### Setting up Your Environment

To effectively develop, build, and run the BigBlueButton API applications you must properly set up your
environment. At minimum, [Go](https://go.dev/), [golangci-lint](https://golangci-lint.run/),
[Docker](https://www.docker.com/), [the protocol buffer compiler](https://protobuf.dev/installation/), 
[the Go protocol buffer compiler](https://protobuf.dev/reference/go/go-generated-opaque/), and [gRPC for Go](https://grpc.io/docs/languages/go/quickstart/) 
must be installed for everything to function properly.

If you are on Ubuntu you may run `setup-dev-env.sh` located in the top-level directory of `bbb-api`
to easily setup your environment.

### Building and Running APIs Locally

Each API has a separate `main.go` file that must be built against and executed. The main entry point for
each API is located in a subdirectory of `/cmd`. For example, to build a local version of the Meeting 
API you can run these commands from the top-level directory of `/bbb-api`:
```
go mod vendor
go build ./cmd/meeting-api
```
Akka Apps should be running locally alongside these APIs for everything to function properly. You can run
Akka Apps from the `bbb-akka-apps` directory using `run-dev.sh`.

The new API can be accessed at `https://YOUR-DOMAIN/getMeetings?checksum=123`.

Update each APIs configuration with your security salt which can be obtained using `bbb-conf --salt`.

### Linting

Linting is done using `golangci-lint`. Run linting by using:
```
golangci-lint run
```

### Unit Testing

Run unit tests with:
```
go clean -testcache
go test ./...
```

### Building the Docker Images

Each API service has its own Dockerfile following the naming convention `Dockerfile-{service}` where `service` is substituted with the
API name. As an example, to build the Docker image for the meeting API use the following commands:
```
go mod vendor
docker build -f Dockerfile-meeting -t imdt/bigbluebutton-api-meeting:latest .
```

### Integration Testing

Integration tests are specific to individual APIs and build tags are used to differentiate the tests. For example, to run the integration
tests for the meeting API against the built docker image use:
```
go test ./test/... --tags=integration,meeting
```